# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — Documentation Guide

> **⚠️ AUTHORITY: The rules in this file govern all documentation changes.**
> When any instruction in any other document conflicts with a rule here, this file wins.

This document defines how project documentation is structured and maintained. It applies to every
human contributor, AI assistant, and automated process that reads or writes documentation here.

**Scope:** any file whose primary purpose is human-readable explanation — architecture docs, design
specs, flow descriptions, feature specs, session plans, contributor guides, API references, decision
logs.

**Not in scope:** source-code comments, CI configuration, build scripts.

---

## 1. Directory structure

All documentation lives under `docs/`. This is a constraint, not a preference: scattered docs cannot
be audited, and a reader who has to search for the entry point does not find it.

**Two parts of the tree are fixed. The rest is this project's to decide.**

Fixed, for every project:

```
docs/
├── <entry point>        ← SINGLE ENTRY POINT — read first for every task
└── <this file>          ← governs all doc changes
```

Everything else follows from what this project is. The shapes below are starting points drawn from
real repositories, not a menu to adopt wholesale:

| If this project is | Its tree usually also holds |
|---|---|
| A published package (library) | the API reference for its public surface; the changelog and the deprecation ledger; `decisions/` as a **directory**, because a long-lived library accumulates more decisions than one file holds |
| A deployed API or data service | the API doc and the cross-cutting API standard; the deployment and production-readiness docs; the error and rate-limit conventions every route must follow |

